/*
  tx_rekey: transfer of control over an object.

  Rekey is how control of an object moves between parties. An object's
  `key` field is the secret that authorizes spending it: the nullifier
  and the spend endorsement both hash it (see EndorseSpend in
  txlib.podlang), so whoever knows `key` and only they can consume the
  object's current state. Rekey mutates an object to an otherwise
  identical state under a new key.

  This lives in its own module, separate from txlib.podlang, for the
  same reason the tx_events primitives do: every class's type guard
  references Rekey, so its hash is baked into every plugin module hash.
  Keeping it out of txlib.podlang means churn in the replay and
  finalize predicates does not invalidate plugin manifests. Edits here
  are interface-breaking, so the module's batch id is pinned by a test
  in mod.rs.

  Rekey is generic over the object's class: the `type` argument is the
  class's own guard hash, so a class opts in by adding one branch to
  its guard,

    IsWoodPick(obj, state_header StateHeader, chain_start, chain_end) = OR(
      ...
      rk::Rekey(obj, chain_start, chain_end, @self_predicate(IsWoodPick))
    )

  and a class that should not be transferable simply omits it.
*/

use module 0xTX_EVENTS_MODULE_HASH as tx

// Two-party transfer in a single action.
//
// The two DictUpdate clauses walk the object's key through a publicly
// known sentinel:
//
//   old  --(key := {})--> mid --(key := new_key)--> new
//
// This split is what lets a transfer happen in one action without
// either party learning the other's key. Only the current owner can
// prove the first clause, because updating `old` requires opening it,
// and only they know `old.key`. The receiver can prove the second
// clause without any help: `mid` is `old` with the key erased, so the
// receiver can reconstruct it from the object's disclosed fields, and
// `new_key` is theirs alone. Neither clause exposes a key --
// DictUpdate's arguments are the two dict commitments, the field name
// and the new value, never the field's previous value, and `new_key`
// is a private wildcard.
//
// The first clause doubles as a disclosure proof: the receiver
// recomputes `mid` from the fields the owner claims the object has,
// and a commitment match proves the owner described every non-key
// field honestly.
//
// `mid` is a witness and must never become an event object. Splitting
// this into two chain events (erase the key, then set it) would make
// the intermediate's nullifier publicly computable, since its key is
// the sentinel, and two transfers of an object whose other fields
// happen to match would then collide on that nullifier and be
// rejected as a double-spend.
//
// DictUpdate requires the key to already exist and touches nothing
// else, so `type` and `stable_identifier` survive: the object stays
// under the same guard, and TxMutate's identity clause holds without
// any extra constraint here.
Rekey(new, chain_start, chain_end, type, private: old, mid, new_key) = AND(
  DictUpdate(old, "key", {}, mid)
  DictUpdate(mid, "key", new_key, new)
  tx::TxMutate(chain_start, chain_end, old, new, type)
)
